{:edb/db-before-t 1272 :edb/db-after-t 1273 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534585 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:39:08.577Z" 13194139534585 true] [17592186087496 1100 "Suppose that the population $P(t)$ of a town after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}P} {\\textrm{d}t} = 0.02P.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $500$ people in the town, what will be the town's population in $3$ years?" 13194139534585 true] [17592186087496 1100 "Suppose that the population $P(t)$ of a town after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}P} {\\textrm{d}t} = 0.02P$.\n\nIf there are currently $500$ people in the town, what will be the town's population in $3$ years?" 13194139534585 false] [17592186087496 1145 "We note that this is an exponential growth differential equation with $r = 0.02$. Therefore, the solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = P_0\\,e^{0.02t}\n$$\n\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the population is currently $500$, so we have $P_0 = 500$.\n\nTherefore, the population of the town at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 500\\,e^{0.02t}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the population after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3) &= 500e^{0.02 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= 500e^{0.06} \\\\\n&\\approx 531\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the population will be approximately $531$ after $3$ years." 13194139534585 false] [17592186087496 1145 "We note that this is an exponential growth differential equation with $r = 0.02$. Therefore, the solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = P_0\\,e^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the population is currently $500,$ so we have $P_0 = 500.$ \n\nTherefore, the population of the town at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 500\\,e^{0.02t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the population after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =500\\,{e}^{0.02\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =500\\,{e}^{0.06} \\\\  & ≈531\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the population will be approximately $531$ after $3$ years." 13194139534585 true] [17592186087561 1145 "The rate $N'(t)$ at which the number of followers increases is directly proportional to the number of followers $N$. Therefore, we can model the number of followers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = rN\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is measured in months, and $r > 0$ is the growth rate.\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0\\,e^{rt}\n$$\n\nwhere $N_0$ is the initial number of followers.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $600\\,000$ followers, so we have\n\n$$\nN_0 = 600\\,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of followers $N$ after $t$ months is\n\n$$\nN(t) = 600\\,000 \\, e^{rt}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the growth rate, we substitute\n\n$$\nN(3)=780\\,000\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(3) &= 600000e^{r \\cdot 3} \\\\\n780000 &= 600000e^{3r} \\\\\ne^{3r} &= \\frac{780000}{600000} \\\\\ne^{3r} &= 1.3 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{3r}) &= \\ln (1.3) \\\\\n3r &= \\ln (1.3) \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln (1.3) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.087455\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr\\approx 9\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139534585 false] [17592186087561 1145 "The rate $N'(t)$ at which the number of followers increases is directly proportional to the number of followers $N.$ Therefore, we can model the number of followers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = rN,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is measured in months, and $r > 0$ is the growth rate.\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0\\,e^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere $N_0$ is the initial number of followers.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $600\\,000$ followers, so we have $N_0 = 600\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of followers $N$ after $t$ months is\n\n$$\nN(t) = 600\\,000 \\, e^{rt} .\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the growth rate, we substitute $N(3)=780\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =600\\,000\\,{e}^{r\\cdot 3} \\\\ 780\\,000 & =600\\,000\\,{e}^{3r} \\\\ {e}^{3r} & =\\frac{780\\,000}{600\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{3r} & =1.3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ 3r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ r & ≈0.087\\,54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r\\approx 9\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." 13194139534585 true] [17592186087562 1100 "Suppose that the value $V(t)$ of a particular car can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -0.1V,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the car was first purchased. If the car was purchased for $\\[math]10\\,000,$ what will it be worth after $3$ years?" 13194139534585 true] [17592186087562 1100 "Suppose that the value $V(t)$ of a particular car can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -0.1V$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the car was first purchased. If the car was purchased for $10000$ dollars, what will it be worth after $3$ years?" 13194139534585 false] [17592186087562 1145 "First, notice that the right-hand side of the equation is *negative*, which means that the car's value is *decreasing.*\n\nThis is an exponential decay differential equation with $r = 0.1$. Therefore, the general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 e^{-0.1 t}\n$$\n\nWe know that the car was first purchased for $10000$ dollars. Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_0 = 10\\,000\n$$\n\nand we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nV(t) = 10\\,000 e^{-0.1 t}\n$$\n\nTo find the car's value after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the particular solution above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(3) &= 10000e^{-0.1 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= 10000e^{-0.3} \\\\\n&\\approx 7408\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth approximately $7408$ dollars after three years." 13194139534585 false] [17592186087562 1145 "First, notice that the right-hand side of the equation is *negative*, which means that the car's value is *decreasing.*\n\nThis is an exponential decay differential equation with $r = 0.1.$ Therefore, the general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 e^{-0.1 t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the car was first purchased for $\\[math]10\\,000.$ Therefore, $V_0 = 10\\,000,$ and we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nV(t) = 10\\,000 e^{-0.1 t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the car's value after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the particular solution above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =10\\,000{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =10\\,000{e}^{-0.3} \\\\  & ≈7\\,408.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth approximately $\\[/math]7\\,408$ after three years." 13194139534585 true] [17592186087821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297037 13194139534585 true] [17592186087821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297079 13194139534585 true] [17592186087821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297083 13194139534585 true] [17592186087821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297086 13194139534585 true] [17592186087821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297105 13194139534585 true] [17592186087822 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297044 13194139534585 true] [17592186087822 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297058 13194139534585 true] [17592186087822 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297089 13194139534585 true] [17592186087823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297051 13194139534585 true] [17592186087823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297065 13194139534585 true] [17592186087823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297072 13194139534585 true] [17592186087823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297093 13194139534585 true] [17592186087823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297096 13194139534585 true] [17592186087955 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe number of particles $N(t)$ in a radioactive substance after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dN}{dt} =-3N$.\nIf there were $8500$ particles initially, calculate the number of particles remaining after $2$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534585 false] [17592186087955 1100 "The number of particles $N(t)$ in a radioactive substance after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nIf there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, calculate the number of particles remaining after $2$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534585 true] [17592186087955 1145 "The solution of $N^{\\prime}=-3N$ is $N(t)=Ce^{-3t}$. Since $N(0)=8500$, we obtain $N(2)=8500e^{-6}\\approx21.069$. There are $21$ particles remaining to the nearest integer." 13194139534585 false] [17592186087955 1145 "We model the number of particles in the substance using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=8\\,500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of particles $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of particles when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-6} \\\\  & ≈21.069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $21$ particles remain." 13194139534585 true] [17592186088358 1017 #edb/ref 17592186087078 13194139534585 false] [17592186088358 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297099 13194139534585 true] [17592186088358 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe outstanding amount $L(t)$ that Daphne needs to repay the bank $t$ years after taking out a loan can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dL}{dt} =-0.5L$.\n\nIf she borrowed $32000$ dollars, calculate the debt remaining after $8$ years. Round your answer to the nearest dollar." 13194139534585 false] [17592186088358 1100 "The outstanding amount $L(t)$ that Daphne needs to repay the bank $t$ years after taking out a loan can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5L.\n$$\n\nIf she borrowed $$32\\,000,$ calculate the debt remaining after $8$ years. Round your answer to the nearest dollar." 13194139534585 true] [17592186088358 1145 "The solution of $L^{\\prime}=-0.5L$ is $L(t)=Ce^{-0.5t}$. The initial debt gives $C=32000$, so $L(8)=32000e^{-4}\\approx586.100$. The remaining debt is $586$ dollars to the nearest dollar." 13194139534585 false] [17592186088358 1145 "We model the amount of debt remaining using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5L.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nL(t)={L}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.5t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${L}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that $$32\\,000$ were borrowed, so we have ${L}_{0}=32\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the amount of debt $L$ remaining at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nL(t)=32\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.5t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of debt remaining when $t=8,$ we substitute $t=8$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(8) & =32\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =32\\,000\\,{e}^{-4} \\\\  & ≈586.100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $586$ dollars of debt remain." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297037 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297038 13194139534585 true] [17592186297037 1089 "q-119231" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297037 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534585 true] [17592186297037 1100 "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a certain site's e-mail news service after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.01S.\n$$\n\nIf the site's e-mail news service currently has $10\\,000$ subscribers, calculate the number of subscribers after $10$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297037 1113 #uuid "c40bcda7-b5a6-51e6-b219-ea1070a93d2d" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297037 1145 "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.01S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.01t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the service currently has $10\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=10\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.01t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=10,$ we substitute $t=10$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(10) & =10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.01\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.1} \\\\  & ≈11\\,051.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $11\\,052$ after $10$ weeks." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297039 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297040 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297041 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297042 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297043 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1041 "selection" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297043 13194139534585 true] [17592186297038 1110 #uuid "3f4ac8bb-fa3b-5ae2-9aa7-46f343f9a358" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297039 1034 "14\\,564" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297039 1111 #uuid "70ed1f03-582d-52f4-bea5-294909e97b01" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297040 1034 "10\\,981" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297040 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297040 1111 #uuid "ba740ff2-5a93-5217-a330-7d9123b718ca" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297041 1034 "13\\,002" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297041 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297041 1111 #uuid "39f63ed7-ab7d-53a9-b263-ed5407f6b26f" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297042 1034 "12\\,231" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297042 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297042 1111 #uuid "49ec0a8b-005f-53dc-a28e-ed3000e58c89" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297043 1034 "11\\,052" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297043 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297043 1111 #uuid "90e605a5-9568-5dd0-bbef-c12cb7840fd9" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297044 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297045 13194139534585 true] [17592186297044 1089 "q-119341" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297044 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534585 true] [17592186297044 1100 "The number of sales $S(t)$ of a new tech device $t$ weeks after the start of sales can be modeled by the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1S.\n$$\n\nIf there were $100\\,000$ sales initially, calculate the number of sales after $10$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297044 1113 #uuid "a23ea5e1-0ee0-52e5-9a48-03228f9f384c" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297044 1145 "We model the number of sales using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.1t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $100\\,000$  sales initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=100\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of sales $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.1t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of sales when $t=10,$ we substitute $t=10$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(10) & =100\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =100\\,000\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈36\\,787.94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that there are $36\\,788$ sales after $10$ weeks." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297046 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297047 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297048 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297049 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297050 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1041 "selection" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297050 13194139534585 true] [17592186297045 1110 #uuid "4f14decb-5086-55da-8c82-9a07ed193f9c" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297046 1034 "52\\,009" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297046 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297046 1111 #uuid "c47ea5a8-edf2-52fd-87f7-826960587250" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297047 1034 "73\\,558" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297047 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297047 1111 #uuid "f347ea5a-346a-5114-a80f-82a5223d3312" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297048 1034 "42\\,125" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297048 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297048 1111 #uuid "b28c122f-1012-5fef-adc9-c9a80afa4efb" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297049 1034 "67\\,124" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297049 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297049 1111 #uuid "54853199-f168-53ba-a585-689664f50bf4" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297050 1034 "36\\,788" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297050 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297050 1111 #uuid "656f9fa0-8c9c-5ecf-a867-a7e8aff11624" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297051 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297052 13194139534585 true] [17592186297051 1089 "q-119597" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297051 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534585 true] [17592186297051 1100 "The value $V=V(t)$ of a certain art object $t$ years after its production can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If it was initially worth $$2\\,000,$ and $V(10)=10\\,000$, calculate the value  of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297051 1113 #uuid "138cbf95-75f0-5bc8-9a00-62d7731a150b" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297051 1145 "We model the value of the art object using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that  it was initially worth $$2\\,000,$ so we have ${V}_{0}=2\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the value $V$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nV(t)=2\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $V(10)=10\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(10) & =2\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 10} \\\\ 10\\,000 & =2\\,000\\,{e}^{10k} \\\\ {e}^{10k} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{2\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{10k} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{10k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ 10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ k & ≈0.16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈16%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297053 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297054 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297055 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297056 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297057 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1041 "selection" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297053 13194139534585 true] [17592186297052 1110 #uuid "60f6cf4a-669d-5738-8caa-f91fb9caf015" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297053 1034 "16%" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297053 1111 #uuid "868615d9-0b84-5d22-ac2f-6ea9ef760a37" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297054 1034 "24%" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297054 1111 #uuid "252e8e72-934c-5027-aae8-b120243cebc1" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297055 1034 "11%" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297055 1111 #uuid "3a8a2d31-8f9a-588d-b0cf-6931f36dc212" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297056 1034 "19%" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297056 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297056 1111 #uuid "0508390d-b2e8-5d2f-9bc2-6d2993161ef6" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297057 1034 "30%" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297057 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534585 true] [17592186297057 1111 #uuid "a0f24df6-613d-5557-9ed8-5b16b51e8e5a" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297058 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297059 13194139534585 true] [17592186297058 1089 "q-339591" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297058 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534585 true] [17592186297058 1100 "The mass of a radioactive sample $R(t)$ (in milligrams) after $t$ days can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nIf the sample initially had a mass of $80$ milligrams, calculate its mass after $30$ days. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297058 1113 #uuid "dc797367-c6c0-52d2-9b58-712548881bd1" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297058 1145 "We model the mass of the sample using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nR(t)={R}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${R}_{0}$ is the initial mass.\n\nWe're told that the initial mass of the sample is $80$ milligrams, so we have ${R}_{0}=80.$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the sample $R$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nR(t)=80\\,{e}^{-0.05t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the mass of the sample when $t=30,$ we substitute $t=30$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\b