/ref 17592186045465 13194139534585 true] [17592186297065 1100 "The population of a bacterial culture $N=N(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, and $N(3)=2\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297065 1113 #uuid "60b44c6a-0607-53dc-a99e-dce6dfb84419" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297065 1145 "We model the population of the bacterial culture using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nWe're told that the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the population $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=2\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 2\\,000 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{2\\,000}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =0.462\\,09…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈46%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297067 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297068 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297069 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297070 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297071 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1041 "selection" 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297068 13194139534585 true] [17592186297066 1110 #uuid "62c28a90-1c